Markets spent most of Tuesday pretending that nothing had happened, which in =
this cycle is its own kind of news. The index closed up eleven points, a move=
 so small it barely qualifies as an opinion, and yet the underlying plumbing =
told a much less calm story. Underlying yields rose for the fourth straight s=
ession. That matters because everything in the last two years has been priced=
 off the assumption that borrowing costs had peaked. Four straight up days in=
 the long bond is the market starting to doubt that assumption out loud. The =
energy complex was the other tell. Crude sold off hard into the London close,=
 then quietly recovered in the American afternoon. Nobody at the exchanges wi=
ll say why, and the volume profile suggests a single large seller rather than=
 a change in fundamentals. =E2=80=9CWhat we are hearing from clients is not f=
ear, it is fatigue,=E2=80=9D said one rate strategist at a large dealer bank,=
 speaking on condition of anonymity because he is not authorized to talk to p=
ress. =E2=80=9CEveryone wants the all-clear signal, and nobody wants to be th=
e one who says it too early.=E2=80=9D Equity breadth, meanwhile, was ugly. Ro=
ughly a third of constituents closed lower even as the headline index rose, w=
hich is what a narrowing market looks like before it stops being a narrowing =
market. For the retail investor, none of this is actionable on its own. It is=
, however, a reminder that a portfolio built for a world of falling rates is =
a portfolio with one assumption holding it up, and assumptions are cheaper to=
 test than to repair. Tomorrow brings the inflation print, and with it the fi=
rst chance in six weeks for the bond market to be either vindicated or wrong =
in a way that everyone can agree on. Until then, eleven points.

